{
  "id": "11",
  "pages": 6,
  "body_mappings": [
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        508.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        607.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Go is said to allow more configurations than the universe has atoms, a daunting computational challenge. In October 2015 DeepMind's AlphaGo reached a milestone, becoming the first program to beat a professional on a full board without a handicap. In March 2016 its opponent in a televised Seoul showdown was Lee Sedol, a South Korean strategist with 18 international titles."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        401.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        500.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "In game two, on 10 March, AlphaGo's 37th move was a shoulder hit, a tactic placing a stone adjacent to an opponent's on the diagonal. On the grid's fifth line, it was hardly orthodox. Commentators found the unexpected placement bizarre; one took it for a mistake. Lee left the room, his pause for deliberation lasting over ten minutes."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        313.46141732283473,
        461.8976377952756,
        392.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "DeepMind's diagnostic tools showed AlphaGo had put the odds of a human playing that stone at one in 10,000, a staggering rarity. AlphaGo had first learned to imitate strong amateurs online, then sharpened its play over tens of millions of simulated games against itself. Lee later acknowledged the stone's originality and beauty."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        225.76141732283475,
        461.8976377952756,
        304.96141732283473
      ],
      "text": "The fourth game, on 13 March, reversed the roles in neat symmetry. Lee's 78th move turned the tide, and many professionals heralded it as divine. DeepMind's team computed a professional's likelihood of finding it: about one in 10,000. Lee's only win was a consolation amid AlphaGo's overwhelming 4–1 victory."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        138.06141732283476,
        461.8976377952756,
        217.26141732283475
      ],
      "text": "In November 2019 Lee withdrew from the professional circuit, a farewell he tied to AI's unrivalled dominance. Calling AI an invincible entity, he said the summit was beyond him. Yet his one win came from a stone whose odds, by DeepMind's figures, were as slim as those of AlphaGo's audacious shoulder hit."
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        527.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        607.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "据说，围棋可能出现的局面，比宇宙中的原子还多，这给计算带来了艰巨挑战。2015年10月，DeepMind的AlphaGo达成了一项里程碑：成为首个在全尺寸棋盘上、不让子的条件下击败职业棋手的程序。2016年3月，在首尔一场电视转播的对决中，它的对手是拥有18项国际冠军头衔的韩国围棋高手李世石。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        439.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        519.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "3月10日的第二局，AlphaGo第37手下出一记肩冲，也就是在对方棋子的斜对角相邻处落子。这一手落在从棋盘边缘数起的第五线上，很不合常规。解说员觉得这个出人意料的落点十分怪异，其中一人还以为它下错了。李世石离开对局室，暂停下来思考了十多分钟。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        350.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        430.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "DeepMind的诊断工具显示，AlphaGo曾估计，人类下出这一手的概率只有万分之一，罕见得令人惊叹。AlphaGo起初学习模仿网上实力强劲的业余棋手，随后通过数千万盘模拟自我对弈磨砺棋力。李世石后来也认可了这一手的独创性与美感。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        262.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        342.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "3月13日的第四局，双方的角色巧妙地对调了。李世石第78手扭转局势，许多职业棋手将其誉为“神之一手”。DeepMind团队估算，职业棋手找到这一手的可能性约为万分之一。AlphaGo以4比1取得压倒性胜利，李世石唯一的一胜，则给他带来了一丝安慰。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        173.66141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        253.66141732283472
      ],
      "text": "2019年11月，李世石退出职业棋坛，并将这次告别与人工智能无可匹敌的优势联系起来。他称人工智能是不可战胜的存在，表示自己已无法企及那个巅峰。然而，带来他唯一胜局的那一手，按照DeepMind的数据，人类找到它的概率，和下出AlphaGo那记大胆肩冲的概率一样渺茫。"
    }
  ],
